---
import Since from '../../components/Since.astro';
import Snippet from '../../components/Snippet.astro';
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';

const surface = `class Master {
  volume: number;                       // 0..1, setter ramps over 10 ms
  muted: boolean;                       // keeps volume; unmute restores it
  fadeTo(target: number, seconds: number, curve?: FadeCurve): Promise<void>;

  readonly headroom: number;            // dB
  setHeadroom(db: number): void;        // ramps over 10 ms

  setLimiter(cfg: MasterLimiterConfig | null): void;
  readonly reduction: number;           // limiter gain reduction, dB (<= 0)

  meter(): AudioLevel;                  // { rms, peak }, linear 0..1
}

interface MasterConfig {
  volume?: number;                      // default 1
  mute?: boolean;                       // default false
  headroom?: number;                    // dB, default 0
  limiter?: MasterLimiterConfig;        // off unless present
}

interface MasterLimiterConfig {
  threshold?: number;                   // dB, default -1
  ratio?: number;                       // default 20 (and never lower)
  attack?: number;                      // s, default 0.001
  release?: number;                     // s, default 0.05
}`;

const config = `const engine = createEngine({
  buses: { music: {}, sfx: {}, voice: {} },
  master: {
    volume: 0.9,
    headroom: -3,
    limiter: { threshold: -1 },
  },
});`;

const settings = `// A settings screen: one slider, one mute toggle.
volumeSlider.oninput = () => { engine.master.volume = Number(volumeSlider.value); };
muteToggle.onchange = () => { engine.master.muted = muteToggle.checked; };

// Muting keeps the volume. Unmute comes back to wherever the slider was.
engine.master.muted = true;
engine.master.volume = 0.4;   // stored while muted
engine.master.muted = false;  // ramps to 0.4`;

const fade = `// Fade the whole game out before a route change, then close.
await engine.master.fadeTo(0, 0.6, 'equal-power');
await engine.close();`;

const limiter = `// Turn the limiter on for the big-win sequence only.
engine.master.setLimiter({ threshold: -2, release: 0.1 });
await playBigWin();
engine.master.setLimiter(null);

// A clip light for a debug overlay.
requestAnimationFrame(function draw() {
  const { peak } = engine.master.meter();
  clipLed.classList.toggle('on', peak > 0.98 || engine.master.reduction < -3);
  requestAnimationFrame(draw);
});`;

const migrate = `// v1
const { rms, peak } = engine.masterMeter();

// v2
const { rms, peak } = engine.master.meter();`;
---
<Docs
  title="Master bus"
  description="The last stage before the speakers. It holds the headroom, the master fader, an optional soft limiter, and a meter on everything the engine plays."
  eyebrow="Concept"
>
  <p>
    Six buses that each peak at a sensible level can still add up to a clip
    when they all fire on the same frame, which in a slot game is exactly the
    big-win frame. And every game has a settings screen with one volume
    slider and one mute switch that should move everything at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every bus output feeds the master. The chain is headroom gain, then the
    master fader (volume and mute), then the limiter if one is set, then
    <code>ctx.destination</code>. There is one per engine, reached as
    <code>engine.master</code> <Since v="2.0" />. Reading it builds the
    audio graph, so do it after <code>unlock()</code> or accept that the
    context gets created on that read.
  </p>

  <h2>API surface</h2>
  <Snippet code={surface} lang="ts" title="Master" />

  <h2>Configure at creation</h2>
  <p>
    <code>volume</code> and <code>mute</code> <Since v="2.0" /> join
    <code>headroom</code> and <code>limiter</code> on
    <code>MasterConfig</code>. Volume and headroom are separate stages on
    purpose. Headroom is a mix decision you set once, volume is the player's.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={config} lang="ts" title="createEngine({ master })" />

  <h2>Volume and mute <Since v="2.0" /></h2>
  <p>
    Both setters ramp over 10 ms, so a slider dragged quickly never clicks.
    Mute is a flag on top of volume. The volume is remembered while muted,
    and a volume set while muted applies when you unmute.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={settings} lang="ts" />

  <h3>Fades</h3>
  <p>
    <code>fadeTo(target, seconds, curve)</code> resolves when the fade is
    done on the audio clock. While muted it only stores the target.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={fade} lang="ts" />

  <h2>Headroom and limiter</h2>
  <p>
    The limiter wraps a <code>DynamicsCompressorNode</code> with a fast
    attack and a ratio of at least 20. It has no lookahead, so a sharp
    enough transient still gets past it. Leave a few dB of headroom as well
    rather than relying on it. <code>setLimiter(null)</code> takes it out of
    the chain; <code>reduction</code> reads 0 when there isn't one.
  </p>
  <Snippet code={limiter} lang="ts" />

  <h2>Metering</h2>
  <p>
    <code>engine.master.meter()</code> returns <code>{'{'} rms, peak {'}'}</code>
    on the master input, the same shape as <code>bus.meter()</code> and
    <code>voice.level()</code>. The analyser is attached on the first call
    and costs nothing before it.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <div class="note-title">engine.masterMeter() is deprecated in v2</div>
    It still works and returns the same reading. Switch to
    <code>engine.master.meter()</code>; the old method goes in a later
    major. See <a href="/guides/migrating-to-v2/">Migrating to v2</a>.
  </div>
  <Snippet code={migrate} lang="ts" />

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/concepts/bus/">Bus</a> is the same idea one level down.</li>
    <li><a href="/fx/compressor/">Compressor</a> for dynamics on a single bus.</li>
    <li><a href="/concepts/engine/">Engine</a> lists the rest of <code>EngineConfig</code>.</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
